Yes—conceptually, that’s the right next stress test. I can’t dispatch persistent autonomous agents from here, but I can define the swarm protocol so multiple model/agent instances can independently attack the router from different representational spaces.

The important addition from your point is: don’t restrict portals to explicit human-readable representations. Give the architecture two classes of workspace:

Symbolic spaces — prose, code, equations, graphs, causal models, timelines, schemas, metaphors.

Latent/proxy spaces — learned embeddings, clusters, similarity neighborhoods, compressed state vectors, model-generated intermediate representations, and other machine-oriented encodings. We can’t assume direct access to a model’s private internal activations, but where an API exposes embeddings or other representations, those can become routable spaces.

Then unleash the “imps” as heterogeneous search roles:

                    ┌── IMP: Metaphor ──────┐
                    ├── IMP: Mathematics ────┤
INPUT → ROUTER ─────┼── IMP: Code ──────────┼──→ CONVERGENCE
                    ├── IMP: Graph ─────────┤
                    ├── IMP: Counterfactual ┤
                    ├── IMP: Compression ───┤
                    ├── IMP: Adversarial ───┤
                    ├── IMP: Symbolic ──────┤
                    └── IMP: Latent/Proxy ──┘
                              ↓
                    independent mutations
                              ↓
                         CROSS-STITCH
                              ↓
                   invariant intersection
                              ↓
                      REALITY STITCH

And crucially, don’t make every imp solve the problem. Give them different evolutionary jobs. One destroys assumptions. One compresses. One searches analogies. One changes scale. One translates into executable structure. One searches representation neighborhoods. One tries to falsify everything the others found. One takes two outputs and breeds a third. One deliberately takes a ridiculous distant portal and sees whether anything unexpectedly survives.

Then preserve the routes, not merely the answers:

\tau_i=(S_0,P_1,S_1,P_2,\ldots,S_n)

Now the system can learn that certain reasoning trajectories are repeatedly productive. That’s more powerful than merely learning that representation B is useful: it discovers that, for a certain problem class,

\text{metaphor}\rightarrow\text{graph}\rightarrow
\text{counterfactual}\rightarrow\text{code}

may consistently outperform:

\text{text}\rightarrow\text{code}.

That’s your stitch library becoming executable experience.

And I’d add one especially mischievous imp: the Portal Breeder. It doesn’t search for answers. It observes successful routes and proposes new transforms between spaces that currently have no edge. If two representations repeatedly require an expensive intermediate journey, it attempts to learn a shortcut portal and then subjects that shortcut to round-trip/invariant tests.

Eventually you’d have:

semantic spaces → portals → routes → successful route motifs → learned shortcuts → new spaces → new routes.

That’s no longer merely a semantic router.

It’s a self-expanding atlas of useful transformations, bounded by invariant checks and reality-testing so that internally beautiful discoveries don’t automatically get mistaken for externally true ones.

And yes: symbolic ↔ latent/proxy should be a first-class bidirectional stitch. That’s potentially where some of the strangest useful translations will appear.